1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,294
Concrete bunkers. Huge guns.

2
00:00:03,295 --> 00:00:10,000
A gauntlet of piercing, blocking and exploding obstacles.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,115
Plus thousands of well-armed German soldiers.

4
00:00:13,116 --> 00:00:19,000
The only way to defeat Hitler's Germany was to breach this daunting line of defenses.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Today, scattered along the coast of Western Europe, lie remnants of the vast fortifications

7
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:50,460
erected during World War II to protect Hitler's Germany against an Allied invasion.

8
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:59,540
In France, along the Normandy coast, the beaches are quiet now, except for the winds that blow

9
00:00:59,541 --> 00:01:02,720
off the English Channel and the waves that brush against the shore.

10
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:10,200
Guns and tanks that remain are rusted, are on display in museums that safeguard the memory

11
00:01:10,201 --> 00:01:13,140
of what happened here on June 6, 1944.

12
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:15,760
D-Day.

13
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,520
It's the most important day of the 20th century.

14
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,420
And everything before that led up to D-Day.

15
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:30,080
Everything since has been a consequence of what happened on D-Day.

16
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:35,240
It was the day that the Allied armies of America, Britain, and Canada

17
00:01:35,241 --> 00:01:38,660
breached the barrier that made an entire continent a fortress,

18
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:40,680
Hitler's Atlantic Wall.

19
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,280
The Atlantic Wall was one of the great walls of history.

20
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:47,280
There are many great walls, the Great Wall of China,

21
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:51,880
the Roman Wall, which walled off the barbarians from the Roman Empire,

22
00:01:51,881 --> 00:01:56,120
and then the long, long wall that Hitler built from Norway,

23
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:01,020
the entire Atlantic coast to try to block Europe off from the Americans and the British.

24
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:06,760
The Atlantic Wall defenses extended along nearly 3,000 miles of coastline,

25
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:11,000
from the Arctic Circle to the Pyrenees Mountains along Spain's northern border.

26
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:17,520
The Atlantic Wall was not a continuous barrier, but rather a series of fortified locations.

27
00:02:17,521 --> 00:02:24,480
between the man-made defenses precipitous cliffs and rocky shorelines provided natural barriers to

28
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:33,120
keep invaders at bay the most imposing features along the Atlantic wall were massive concrete

29
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:38,580
bunkers built to protect the all-important guns installed there to destroy an approaching invasion

30
00:02:38,581 --> 00:02:44,347
fleet. The heaviest artillery was 40.6 centimeter gun.

31
00:02:44,348 --> 00:02:48,940
There were several of them, seven in Norway

32
00:02:48,941 --> 00:02:50,796
and three in France.

33
00:02:50,797 --> 00:02:58,220
There were navy guns shooting quite precise over a range of maximum 30-35 miles.

34
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:06,280
Bunkers were also designed to serve as observation posts, ammunition stockpiles,

35
00:03:06,281 --> 00:03:13,280
communication centers, troops' quarters, and to store supplies and food to sustain the men stationed on the wall.

36
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:23,280
Here, south of Ostend in Belgium, lies a typical stretch of Atlantic wall construction.

37
00:03:23,281 --> 00:03:28,280
What you see there is the fire control post of a coastal battery.

38
00:03:28,281 --> 00:03:34,280
What you see here is an emplacement with an anti-aircraft gun.

39
00:03:34,281 --> 00:03:42,367
craft gun and very special because it's still there, a system of covered trenches.

40
00:03:42,368 --> 00:03:43,068
These

41
00:03:43,069 --> 00:03:49,520
are normal trenches but covered, protecting soldiers running forth in these corridors

42
00:03:49,521 --> 00:03:55,100
and also protecting the site from the air.

43
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:59,280
To help obscure the bunkers and guns from Allied aerial reconnaissance, the Germans

44
00:03:59,281 --> 00:04:01,988
blanketed them with camouflage netting.

45
00:04:01,989 --> 00:04:05,460
From the air, Allied observers could see the array

46
00:04:05,461 --> 00:04:09,296
of obstacles designed to block an invasion on the beaches.

47
00:04:09,297 --> 00:04:11,280
But hidden beneath the water's

48
00:04:11,281 --> 00:04:16,820
surface often lurked a menacing gauntlet of death and destruction.

49
00:04:16,821 --> 00:04:22,320
During the war, the challenge for the Germans was to block every possible attempt of invasion

50
00:04:22,321 --> 00:04:23,393
by the Allies.

51
00:04:23,394 --> 00:04:28,760
And for that purpose, they didn't only build thousands of bunkers, but

52
00:04:28,761 --> 00:04:35,640
They covered literally the beach with hundreds of thousands of beach obstacles.

53
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:41,200
Not only obstacles, but also thousands of kilometers of bulk of the wire

54
00:04:41,201 --> 00:04:43,820
has been used on the Atlantic Wall.

55
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,740
Between the bunkers, on the fields, and do not forget the mines.

56
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:51,700
About more than six million mines have been placed all over the coast.

57
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,840
The Atlantic Wall was a fortification of such magnitude

58
00:04:55,841 --> 00:05:01,760
that only the most ambitious and audacious attack in the history of warfare could possibly defeat it.

59
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:09,320
During the first few years of World War II, German defeat seemed almost impossible.

60
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:12,480
Adolf Hitler's war machine appeared unstoppable.

61
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:20,920
In the 1930s, the Fuhrer had seemed an answered prayer to a Germany left battered and bankrupt after World War I.

62
00:05:20,921 --> 00:05:27,660
Hitler promised a way out of despair through conquest.

63
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:33,000
In 1939, he unleashed his army against the nations of Europe.

64
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:37,140
In March, Czechoslovakia fell.

65
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,560
In September, Hitler's forces poured into Poland.

66
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,980
Britain and France at last declared war on Germany.

67
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,880
But they could do little to halt the Nazi blitzkrieg.

68
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:56,180
Hitler's armies marched almost unchecked in the first years, starting with Poland, cleared

69
00:05:56,181 --> 00:06:00,565
his eastern frontier, then Scandinavia, Denmark and Norway.

70
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:03,240
From there on into the low countries

71
00:06:03,241 --> 00:06:06,980
and France, most of Western Europe fell under his sway.

72
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:13,780
If you had penetrated South France, you would roll and roll and roll and roll, and it was

73
00:06:13,781 --> 00:06:20,400
almost impossible for your opponent to stop you.

74
00:06:20,401 --> 00:06:25,540
After a year of conquests, all that separated Britain from Nazi domination were the frigid

75
00:06:25,541 --> 00:06:28,220
waters of the English Channel.

76
00:06:28,221 --> 00:06:33,140
But for England, the channel was still an effective moat.

77
00:06:33,141 --> 00:06:38,500
In 1940, to prepare the way for an invasion of Britain, Hitler's Luftwaffe attempted to

78
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:42,420
bomb England into submission.

79
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:44,860
raining death from the skies in the Battle of Britain.

80
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:53,640
Germany's elite air force outnumbered Britain's more than three to one.

81
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:58,620
But 700 British fighter planes fiercely maintained air supremacy over England.

82
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,280
And after four months, Hitler gave up his plans of invasion.

83
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:09,700
Then he had the choice, attack the English supply system with everything that he had,

84
00:07:09,701 --> 00:07:12,700
or should he turn upon his other potential enemy, Russia?

85
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:14,280
He chose Russia.

86
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:22,780
In June 1941, German forces flooded east toward the Russian border to begin the assault.

87
00:07:23,580 --> 00:07:27,700
But Hitler now had thousands of miles of Western European coastline to protect.

88
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,920
How could he defend so much territory with most of his troops fighting on the Eastern Front?

89
00:07:33,921 --> 00:07:42,080
expecting to defeat russia quickly hitler planned to defend europe with massive artillery

90
00:07:42,081 --> 00:07:48,320
the germans positioned huge guns around the major ports of belgium holland and france

91
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:55,760
but in the east the russians fought valiantly the germans were stuck in russia bracing for

92
00:07:55,761 --> 00:08:01,280
the approaching russian winter as hitler ordered more troops to subdue the soviets

93
00:08:01,281 --> 00:08:03,840
he needed a new plan to protect the Western Front.

94
00:08:04,700 --> 00:08:09,840
His solution was a wall that would defend every mile of coastline from Denmark to Spain.

95
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:19,000
As the war progressed, Hitler's concept of the wall would grow from a modest defense to an impregnable fortress.

96
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:26,540
What could the Germans do more than defend it with blockhouses, batteries, everything?

97
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:28,900
There were no troops enough.

98
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:31,840
There were no armored divisions enough.

99
00:08:32,220 --> 00:08:34,300
They could make concrete shelters.

100
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:35,260
They had labors.

101
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,060
They had materials to construct those things.

102
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:43,980
The Germans had no other choice than defend Europe with an Atlantic one.

103
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:46,940
Hitler gave the order.

104
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:54,080
The challenge was how to execute it with limited resources, in occupied territory, in the middle of a war.

105
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:59,400
The Germans had been in World War I, and they knew how to dig ditches,

106
00:08:59,401 --> 00:09:02,720
and they knew how to put trenches in place,

107
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:06,180
and they knew the kind of fire they needed to hold a position,

108
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:08,180
and they put all of that to use.

109
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:15,260
The first goal was to fortify ports that could be used by the Allies

110
00:09:15,261 --> 00:09:18,040
to land troops, divisions, armies.

111
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:22,060
So from the northern part of Holland to the southern part of France,

112
00:09:22,061 --> 00:09:25,460
the major ports and U-boat bases were fortified.

113
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:30,320
The Germans concentrated their early construction efforts on the French coast,

114
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:35,320
off a strait called the Pas de Calais, where the English Channel was narrowest.

115
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:40,900
From a practical standpoint, this was the most likely route for the Allied invasion force.

116
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:46,880
From Calais, the Allies could march straight to Germany, only a few hundred miles away.

117
00:09:46,881 --> 00:09:51,220
On December 7, 1941,

118
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:55,240
as the Japanese launched its devastating attack on Pearl Harbor,

119
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:57,700
the face of the war changed abruptly.

120
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:01,100
An enraged America declared war on Japan.

121
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:05,380
Three days later, Hitler declared war on the United States.

122
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,960
And we don't know why. Nothing made him do it.

123
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:09,040
But he did it.

124
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:12,040
And when he did it, he was making a bet

125
00:10:12,041 --> 00:10:16,500
that the Hitler youth are going to always outfight

126
00:10:16,501 --> 00:10:20,900
those soft, effeminate Americans who have been brought up in the Boy Scouts.

127
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:24,920
Although many Americans were anxious to strike back at Japan,

128
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:30,180
the United States and its new allied partners agreed to make Germany's defeat the first priority.

129
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:36,040
The basic strategy was laid out within a month or so after Pearl Harbor.

130
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,620
And that was Europe first, cross the Channel, go to the heart of Germany.

131
00:10:40,621 --> 00:10:43,940
That was our basic strategy from the very beginning.

132
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:52,160
In August 1942, the Allies launched a raid on the French coast at the port of Dieppe,

133
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:58,900
in part to appease the Russians, who were insisting that their Western allies waste no time opening a second European front.

134
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:04,940
The Dieppe raid, executed by 6,100 Canadian, British and American troops,

135
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:09,280
was designed as a one-day attack to inflict enough damage on the coastal defenses

136
00:11:09,281 --> 00:11:12,620
to draw at least some Germans away from the Eastern Front.

137
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:18,240
It turned into an object lesson, revealing their unreadiness to launch a major invasion.

138
00:11:19,380 --> 00:11:25,500
The jet raid was a trial for the Allied forces and something of a surprise for the Germans.

139
00:11:25,700 --> 00:11:34,940
They expected a raid, a landing, but not of that size and not one that was ill-prepared like that.

140
00:11:34,941 --> 00:11:42,660
The attack on the heavily fortified port city resulted in more than 4,000 Allied casualties.

141
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:46,440
And almost 1,500 Canadians were taken prisoner.

142
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:55,620
Tanks had bogged down on the shore, unable to gain traction on a beach not of hard-packed sand, but loose stones.

143
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:03,960
The Allies realized that launching a full-scale invasion against a heavily fortified port could spell disaster.

144
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:11,780
The only alternative was to design and build new tools that would enable them to successfully storm Europe's open beaches.

145
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:16,740
The Germans could be sure of two things in late 1942.

146
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:20,680
First, the Allies would launch their invasion from England.

147
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:27,860
And second, it would take months or even years for them to make the preparations necessary to avoid another Dieppe.

148
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:33,740
This would provide Hitler ample time to build his unreachable Atlantic Wall.

149
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:43,300
To fortify the Atlantic Wall, the Germans used more than a million metric tons of steel,

150
00:12:43,301 --> 00:12:48,500
enough to construct 60 skyscrapers, each the size of the Chrysler Building.

151
00:12:48,501 --> 00:12:52,300
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

152
00:12:52,301 --> 00:13:01,620
In 1943, the Atlantic Wall became an important first line of warning and defense for the

153
00:13:01,621 --> 00:13:07,060
the Germans against the ever-increasing Allied bombing raids flying closer and closer to Berlin.

154
00:13:07,061 --> 00:13:14,180
While the big guns, aimed at the sea, waited silently for a naval invasion, the anti-aircraft

155
00:13:14,181 --> 00:13:18,300
guns under the flight paths of the bombers roared during repeated action.

156
00:13:18,301 --> 00:13:27,060
In 1943, when the Allies bombed Germany on a very large scale, a huge amount of Allied

157
00:13:27,061 --> 00:13:33,840
bombers came over every evening and when they came back from Germany they were

158
00:13:33,841 --> 00:13:39,000
not allowed to land in England with bombs on board so they dropped their

159
00:13:39,001 --> 00:13:47,700
bombs sometimes in sea and sometimes on the battery itself the resulting damage

160
00:13:47,701 --> 00:13:51,060
to the exposed artillery compelled the Germans to launch a new phase of

161
00:13:51,061 --> 00:13:55,200
of construction on the wall.

162
00:13:55,201 --> 00:14:00,820
Workers built huge concrete bunkers with walls three and a half meters thick.

163
00:14:00,821 --> 00:14:06,320
So thick they could absorb direct bomb hits with virtually no damage.

164
00:14:06,321 --> 00:14:10,180
The Atlantic Wall became the largest construction project of the entire war.

165
00:14:10,181 --> 00:14:15,940
An awful lot of the Atlantic Wall incidentally was made by Frenchmen who were under the

166
00:14:15,941 --> 00:14:21,060
point of a gun working for the Germans, helping to build this wall, to pour the cement, to

167
00:14:21,061 --> 00:14:23,180
put in the steel reinforcing rods.

168
00:14:23,181 --> 00:14:29,700
A private German firm, Organization TOT, supervised much of the wall's construction.

169
00:14:29,701 --> 00:14:35,900
Yeah, the TOT organization was a semi-governmental organization, a contractor.

170
00:14:35,901 --> 00:14:42,840
Started with the Autobahnen project, then was called in for the West Wall in Germany,

171
00:14:42,841 --> 00:14:47,280
And after that, the Atlantic Wall.

172
00:14:47,281 --> 00:14:53,280
By mid-1942, construction crews were pouring more than 200,000 cubic meters of concrete

173
00:14:53,281 --> 00:14:57,560
every month.

174
00:14:57,561 --> 00:15:03,520
The most heavily fortified portion of the entire Atlantic Wall was a pet project of Hitler's.

175
00:15:03,521 --> 00:15:07,220
Ironically, it had little strategic significance.

176
00:15:07,221 --> 00:15:14,220
The Germans captured four British Channel Islands, namely Jersey, Currency, Alderney and Sark.

177
00:15:14,221 --> 00:15:18,220
It was the only part of Great Britain that they occupied.

178
00:15:18,221 --> 00:15:22,220
So the occupation of the Channel Islands was a big favor for him.

179
00:15:22,221 --> 00:15:24,220
He did two things.

180
00:15:24,221 --> 00:15:33,220
First of all, he fortified them very heavily with bunkers all over the places, with batteries, with huge guns, with anti-aircraft guns.

181
00:15:33,221 --> 00:15:36,220
It could be a base for the Allies to jump onto France.

182
00:15:36,221 --> 00:15:42,540
And secondly, it was propaganda because it was the only part of Great Britain that it occupied.

183
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:48,680
Once organization TOT finished its construction on the islands,

184
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:52,080
it was able to give the mainland coastal defenses its full attention.

185
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:54,640
Its overall output was enormous.

186
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:58,000
Germans are very good in statistics.

187
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:03,880
In the total of the Atlantic Wall, some 11,000, 12,000 bunkers were made,

188
00:16:03,881 --> 00:16:10,840
heavy bunkers with a consumption of 10, 11, 12 million cubic meters of concrete.

189
00:16:12,780 --> 00:16:17,340
But for Adolf Hitler, the massive fortifications still weren't enough.

190
00:16:18,420 --> 00:16:23,420
He decided the Atlantic Wall should consist of 15,000 defensive posts along the coast,

191
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:29,580
manned by over 300,000 soldiers and backed by over 150,000 reserves.

192
00:16:29,581 --> 00:16:36,900
but organization taught anticipated that it would be able to complete only 6 000 defensive posts by

193
00:16:36,901 --> 00:16:45,120
june 1943 hitler's goals for troop deployments on the wall were also falling short and units

194
00:16:45,121 --> 00:16:52,260
already stationed there varied greatly in strength training and experience the major forces of the

195
00:16:52,261 --> 00:16:57,700
German army, the young guys, strong guys, were in action in the desert and on the Eastern

196
00:16:57,701 --> 00:16:58,401
Front.

197
00:16:58,402 --> 00:17:03,020
Now to populate the Atlantic wall, they used the older guys and very young guys

198
00:17:03,021 --> 00:17:10,020
and also foreign volunteers like Russians, Italians and all that kind of people.

199
00:17:10,021 --> 00:17:13,868
Germany's best weaponry was reserved for the Eastern Front.

200
00:17:13,869 --> 00:17:15,760
The artillery of the Atlantic

201
00:17:15,761 --> 00:17:19,760
The wall was supplemented with captured guns.

202
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:32,760
This is a Belgian gun, 12-centimeter gun, used by the Belgian army in very small quantities.

203
00:17:32,761 --> 00:17:37,760
And it was captured by the Germans in May 1940.

204
00:17:37,761 --> 00:17:40,760
And there was just one problem with these guns.

205
00:17:40,761 --> 00:17:44,760
There was a very limited stock of ammunition for the Germans.

206
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:53,380
For the German soldiers stationed along the Atlantic Wall, military duties took a backseat to construction projects.

207
00:17:53,920 --> 00:18:00,560
It's very, very remarkable that in a short time, the Germans built a lot of constructions.

208
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:04,980
Little bunkers, but also very big bunkers. It's remarkable.

209
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:08,620
But maintaining momentum became increasingly difficult.

210
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:15,260
The Allied air campaign devastating Germany forced workers along the Atlantic Wall back to the fatherland.

211
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:18,420
where they went to work rebuilding a crumbling infrastructure.

212
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:24,980
The Germans suffered a further setback after the British general Bernard Montgomery

213
00:18:24,981 --> 00:18:30,720
scored an important victory over General Erwin Rommel, the Desert Fox, in North Africa.

214
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,940
Victorious Allied troops there were a new threat,

215
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:39,060
poised near France's vulnerable Mediterranean coast.

216
00:18:40,060 --> 00:18:43,100
In response, the Germans stretched their resources thinner,

217
00:18:43,101 --> 00:18:46,720
fortifying some 400 miles of Mediterranean shoreline.

218
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:53,440
In late 1943, with the possibility of invasion growing with each passing day,

219
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:57,560
Hitler asked Field Marshal Rommel to evaluate the coastal defenses.

220
00:18:58,440 --> 00:19:03,480
My father had among the German generals the greatest experience

221
00:19:03,481 --> 00:19:06,420
in fighting with the British and American troops.

222
00:19:06,421 --> 00:19:13,180
and so he received the order to inspect the defense facilities.

223
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:16,880
Rommel toured the wall from Norway to the Pyrenees.

224
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:22,520
He examined mile after mile of bunkers, gun emplacements, fortifications

225
00:19:22,521 --> 00:19:24,820
and found them woefully inadequate.

226
00:19:27,340 --> 00:19:29,840
Something had to be done and quickly.

227
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:33,940
There was too much coastline to protect and too little time.

228
00:19:33,941 --> 00:19:40,940
The race was on to bolster the Atlantic Wall before the Allies appeared on the horizon.

229
00:19:40,941 --> 00:19:50,940
Dutch architect Jaap Penrath smuggled 400 Jewish men to safety by convincing the Nazis they were slave laborers for the Atlantic Wall.

230
00:19:50,941 --> 00:19:55,940
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

231
00:19:55,941 --> 00:20:05,120
In early 1944, the Germans began to bolster the porous Atlantic Wall defenses.

232
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:10,460
German Field Marshal Erwin Rommel's plans went beyond more bunkers and guns.

233
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:16,180
Miles and miles of barbed wire soon ensnared the coast,

234
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:22,040
and a grotesque assortment of obstacles were placed on the beaches and concealed by high tides,

235
00:20:22,041 --> 00:20:27,040
all designed to destroy, or at least slow down, landing craft,

236
00:20:27,440 --> 00:20:30,860
so machine guns and artillery had more time to decimate the invaders.

237
00:20:31,860 --> 00:20:34,500
Now this is a nutcracker mine.

238
00:20:35,240 --> 00:20:46,160
It's a very simple device, assembled from steel girders and with a piece of rail.

239
00:20:46,161 --> 00:20:51,460
These were placed on the beach between the high tide and the low tide.

240
00:20:52,040 --> 00:21:01,140
When a boat hit the obstacle, this rail exploded a large shell that was attached to the device.

241
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:07,940
Simpler obstacles, nicknamed hedgehogs by the Allies, were three metal girders bolted together.

242
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:12,340
They were first made by the Czechs to help protect their border from the Germans.

243
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:19,300
During the war, these hedgehogs were brought over by the Germans in large quantities

244
00:21:19,301 --> 00:21:22,280
and placed on the beaches along the Atlantic wall.

245
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:29,340
Sometimes they were just meant to stop landing boats.

246
00:21:29,980 --> 00:21:33,380
Sometimes they had mines or other devices attached,

247
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:36,380
just as these tetrahedra here.

248
00:21:36,381 --> 00:21:39,180
You can find them in concrete.

249
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:43,540
Usually they had the teller mine attached to the top.

250
00:21:43,541 --> 00:21:49,500
Belgian gates were first used in Belgium as anti-tank barriers.

251
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:53,240
The Germans saw the value of making them part of the Atlantic wall.

252
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:58,260
The almost elegant gate-like design was actually a sturdy steel construction.

253
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:01,220
Placed individually, they offered little resistance.

254
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:04,960
But when linked together by the coupling hinges on each side,

255
00:22:04,961 --> 00:22:09,120
they created an effective barrier against troops and armored vehicles.

256
00:22:09,121 --> 00:22:16,040
Rulers at the base of the gates allowed the barrier as a whole to flex instead of break

257
00:22:16,041 --> 00:22:18,680
And withstand a tremendous amount of force

258
00:22:18,681 --> 00:22:24,120
In their desperate effort to block or blow up any invader

259
00:22:24,121 --> 00:22:28,080
The Germans added a thicket of the most primitive obstacles imaginable

260
00:22:28,081 --> 00:22:33,480
The Germans literally raided the woods in Belgium and France

261
00:22:33,481 --> 00:22:38,800
And thousands of these wooden posts were placed on the beaches

262
00:22:38,801 --> 00:22:49,700
On top of it, usually they placed a teller mine or a shell, and some of these devices could even rip off the hull of the boat.

263
00:22:50,700 --> 00:23:00,500
At the end of 1943, about the same time Rommel was assigned to the Atlantic Wall, the Allies were deciding who should lead the unprecedented effort to breach it.

264
00:23:01,460 --> 00:23:06,160
The logical choice was General George Marshall, head of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.

265
00:23:06,700 --> 00:23:11,460
But the job of Supreme Allied Commander went instead to General Dwight Eisenhower.

266
00:23:12,180 --> 00:23:15,560
He was given a very simple set of marching orders.

267
00:23:15,561 --> 00:23:21,140
He will enter the continent of Europe in conjunction with other Allied powers, straight to the heart of Germany.

268
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:27,880
Plans for the invasion of Europe, codenamed Overlord, were well underway before Eisenhower's appointment.

269
00:23:27,881 --> 00:23:34,320
A three-division assault had been proposed that called for an invasion of the beaches

270
00:23:34,321 --> 00:23:40,120
between the ports of Le Havre and Cherbourg.

271
00:23:40,121 --> 00:23:43,940
Eisenhower, consulting with British General Montgomery, the commander of the British and

272
00:23:43,941 --> 00:23:48,560
Canadian ground forces, quickly concluded that the plan was inadequate.

273
00:23:48,561 --> 00:23:55,720
Eisenhower and Montgomery had developed a pretty good static operating procedure and

274
00:23:55,721 --> 00:23:59,280
One of the principles is to fly the sledgehammer.

275
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:02,740
So they took a look at this narrow front.

276
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:04,840
They said it's not enough, both of them.

277
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:06,420
But they were absolutely unanimous.

278
00:24:06,760 --> 00:24:09,360
There had to be five divisions rather than three.

279
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:12,740
An equally crucial decision for the Allies

280
00:24:12,741 --> 00:24:14,560
was selecting their invasion site.

281
00:24:15,300 --> 00:24:17,780
The Allies started with reconnaissances

282
00:24:17,781 --> 00:24:20,800
of the whole French and Belgian coasts,

283
00:24:20,801 --> 00:24:21,980
aircraft reconnaissances,

284
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:25,260
and they sent also out small submarines with divers.

285
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:31,880
They came ashore to take little pieces of the sand to investigate in laboratories

286
00:24:31,881 --> 00:24:38,260
to see if the sand was strong enough to keep tanks and other equipment.

287
00:24:38,860 --> 00:24:45,940
And finally, they've chosen for Normandy because they are, in some part of it, very large sandy beaches.

288
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:49,220
Normandy, however, had major disadvantages.

289
00:24:49,221 --> 00:24:55,700
The Allied armada would have to sail across 120 miles of the English Channel

290
00:24:55,701 --> 00:25:00,020
A distance more than five times farther than a route to Calais

291
00:25:00,021 --> 00:25:05,880
And landing at Normandy would also place the Allies farther from their ultimate target

292
00:25:05,881 --> 00:25:07,000
Germany

293
00:25:07,001 --> 00:25:11,000
Still Normandy offered one distinct advantage

294
00:25:11,001 --> 00:25:15,560
The Germans had not fortified it as heavily as they had other parts of the wall

295
00:25:15,561 --> 00:25:21,760
The Germans assumed that the Allies would need a port very quickly after an invasion

296
00:25:21,761 --> 00:25:25,920
in order to support an invasion deep into the empire of Hitler.

297
00:25:27,100 --> 00:25:32,260
The Allies realized that Normandy was not a good place for that, as did Hitler,

298
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:35,400
but they had ideas to compensate for that.

299
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:37,160
They could bring their ports with them.

300
00:25:39,300 --> 00:25:44,220
All over the British Isles, engineers were secretly building huge concrete caissons,

301
00:25:44,221 --> 00:25:49,660
floating piers and sections of pontoon roadway

302
00:25:49,661 --> 00:25:54,260
these were the building blocks of portable modular harbors code named

303
00:25:54,261 --> 00:26:00,820
mulberries when the invasion began the pieces would be towed to Normandy and

304
00:26:00,821 --> 00:26:06,340
assembled into two functional harbors I think each one of those artificial

305
00:26:06,341 --> 00:26:14,060
harbors could handle up to 10,000 tons a day over the beaches

306
00:26:14,061 --> 00:26:23,350
Workers in America's war factories were busy designing and building a variety of specialized shallow bottom landing craft to enable the quick unloading of men and

307
00:26:23,351 --> 00:26:25,300
equipment right up on the beaches.

308
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:32,460
The largest were 300-foot-long landing ship tanks, or LSTs.

309
00:26:33,560 --> 00:26:38,440
Landing ship infantry, or LSI, could hold as many as 150 soldiers.

310
00:26:38,441 --> 00:26:44,860
The smallest craft which would transport the first wave of troops were called Higgins boats.

311
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:48,780
Named after their inventor in Louisiana.

312
00:26:51,020 --> 00:26:56,360
They needed a shallow draft vessel that they could turn around in a hurry.

313
00:26:57,280 --> 00:27:01,700
Every American who went ashore in North Africa, every American who went ashore in Italy,

314
00:27:01,701 --> 00:27:08,420
and every American who went ashore in the Pacific Theater did so in a boat built in New Orleans by Andrew Higgins.

315
00:27:08,440 --> 00:27:10,040
and Higgins' industry.

316
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:12,720
Higgins faced the daunting challenge

317
00:27:12,721 --> 00:27:14,560
of filling the enormous order of the boats

318
00:27:14,561 --> 00:27:16,080
the Allies needed for the invasion.

319
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:20,520
He moved from a factory

320
00:27:20,521 --> 00:27:22,700
that had 80 people working there

321
00:27:22,701 --> 00:27:25,240
to 30,000 employees.

322
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:28,440
Of whom, about half were women.

323
00:27:29,060 --> 00:27:30,820
And he worked seven days a week,

324
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:32,760
24 hours a day,

325
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:34,980
to turn out those landing craft.

326
00:27:35,400 --> 00:27:36,420
And by God, he did it.

327
00:27:36,421 --> 00:27:46,424
As American factories produced more and more equipment for the Allied war effort, German factories and rail stations continued to crumble beneath the onslaught of

328
00:27:46,425 --> 00:27:49,080
Allied bombers, crippling their war output.

329
00:27:51,020 --> 00:27:57,420
The Allied air campaign had also forced the Luftwaffe back into Germany to defend the fatherland cities against attack.

330
00:27:58,840 --> 00:28:03,140
This meant that the Allies would have almost total air superiority over France.

331
00:28:03,141 --> 00:28:09,200
Rommel now believed that Germany could never win this war

332
00:28:09,201 --> 00:28:14,140
But he saw a strong Atlantic Wall as a way to force a negotiated peace with the Allies

333
00:28:14,141 --> 00:28:19,360
The landmine was Rommel's favorite weapon

334
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:22,920
After using them effectively during the North African campaign

335
00:28:22,921 --> 00:28:27,200
He ordered more than 50 million mines to be installed along the Atlantic Wall

336
00:28:27,201 --> 00:28:30,260
On shore there were many kinds of mines

337
00:28:30,261 --> 00:28:33,900
and some of them were designed with glass cases

338
00:28:33,901 --> 00:28:36,560
so that they were not picked up by metal detectors.

339
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:41,000
This sort of problem with mines, non-magnetic mines,

340
00:28:41,440 --> 00:28:44,580
carries right on to the curse of mines throughout the world today.

341
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:48,160
They had laid mines and they had put out barbed wire

342
00:28:48,161 --> 00:28:51,080
and they had their mortars zeroed in.

343
00:28:51,081 --> 00:28:55,200
Now, the Germans who were there were put to work laying out these obstacles

344
00:28:55,201 --> 00:28:57,960
into the water between the low and the high tide

345
00:28:57,961 --> 00:29:03,040
and preparing for the invasion, and they were all praying to God that it doesn't come here.

346
00:29:04,540 --> 00:29:08,780
Under Rommel's supervision, much of the wall was significantly strengthened.

347
00:29:09,620 --> 00:29:14,620
In six months, over five million mines were installed, far short of his target,

348
00:29:15,080 --> 00:29:17,940
but triple the number planted during the years prior to his arrival.

349
00:29:18,400 --> 00:29:23,860
At the end of 1943, Rommel had considered the Atlantic Wall porous and vulnerable.

350
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:30,260
By the spring of 1944, he was more confident his heavily refortified barrier would hold.

351
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:32,680
But its weak spots still troubled him.

352
00:29:33,560 --> 00:29:40,180
In April 1944, Allied planes began destroying communication and transportation lines near the coast.

353
00:29:41,420 --> 00:29:43,800
A sure sign, an invasion was imminent.

354
00:29:44,940 --> 00:29:50,480
The Germans were not surprised that bombs were falling around Calais, as they still expected an invasion there.

355
00:29:50,481 --> 00:29:56,180
But ominously, the Allies were also dropping bombs on the coast of Normandy.

356
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:03,640
The New Orleans D-Day Museum is just blocks away from the earliest Higgins Boat Factory.

357
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:10,440
Founded by historian Stephen Ambrose, the museum houses the world's largest D-Day oral history collection.

358
00:30:11,680 --> 00:30:14,500
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

359
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:30,980
In the spring of 1944, Allied bombs battered Western France.

360
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:35,580
The devastating attacks deeply troubled German Field Marshal Erwin Rommel,

361
00:30:36,100 --> 00:30:38,960
who was spearheading the construction and defense of the Atlantic Wall.

362
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:46,700
My father believed that German strategy should change fundamentally

363
00:30:46,701 --> 00:30:55,840
because he experienced the efficiency of the Allied Air Force in Africa.

364
00:30:56,900 --> 00:31:00,600
Rommel's deep concerns about Allied air power put him in direct conflict

365
00:31:00,601 --> 00:31:02,840
with German General Gerd van Rundstedt.

366
00:31:03,420 --> 00:31:05,900
There's a strange situation.

367
00:31:06,360 --> 00:31:09,080
Rommel was inspector of the fortifications.

368
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:11,940
Van Rundstedt was his boss.

369
00:31:12,560 --> 00:31:14,640
Rommel wanted to fix the front.

370
00:31:14,641 --> 00:31:22,920
von Unscher was a military of the old school who believed in mobile warfare with maneuvering armies.

371
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:28,740
Rommel knew that despite the speed and mobility of Germany's panzer divisions,

372
00:31:29,380 --> 00:31:31,380
Allied airpower could easily destroy them.

373
00:31:32,100 --> 00:31:34,760
So he decided to take his case to Hitler.

374
00:31:35,980 --> 00:31:42,420
So my father said we cannot afford to fight a mobile battle against the British and Americans.

375
00:31:42,421 --> 00:31:51,460
we should try to win the battle in the first days at the shore, at the coast.

376
00:31:51,880 --> 00:31:53,980
But Hitler never resolved the conflict.

377
00:31:54,860 --> 00:31:56,600
Instead, as he had done before,

378
00:31:57,080 --> 00:32:00,740
he used disagreements between his generals to solidify his own power.

379
00:32:01,480 --> 00:32:04,080
He put himself in charge of the defense plans.

380
00:32:04,800 --> 00:32:08,040
When the invasion came, Hitler would dictate strategy.

381
00:32:08,740 --> 00:32:10,200
Rama was not a Nazi.

382
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:11,940
He was Ronstadt.

383
00:32:12,420 --> 00:32:15,900
that Rommel in fact was trying to convince Hitler

384
00:32:15,901 --> 00:32:19,460
make a surrender now

385
00:32:19,461 --> 00:32:21,500
while Germany still got something to trade

386
00:32:21,501 --> 00:32:25,920
but Hitler was determined to continue the war

387
00:32:25,921 --> 00:32:29,060
for him the war was and always would be

388
00:32:29,061 --> 00:32:30,120
all or nothing

389
00:32:30,121 --> 00:32:35,540
although the Germans were now aware of the Allied build up in England

390
00:32:35,541 --> 00:32:38,700
they still didn't know where the invasion force would land

391
00:32:38,701 --> 00:32:41,920
to keep them guessing

392
00:32:41,921 --> 00:32:46,600
The Allies launched an intricate deception operation called Fortitude.

393
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:53,640
The plan was designed to trick the Germans into believing that the invasion would target the Atlantic Wall at Calais.

394
00:32:55,300 --> 00:33:00,840
The way they did that, they created an artificial army group called First Army Group.

395
00:33:01,380 --> 00:33:06,100
General Patton was there in the United Kingdom and he'd been making speeches and getting himself in trouble.

396
00:33:06,101 --> 00:33:08,460
So everybody knew Patton was there.

397
00:33:08,461 --> 00:33:13,260
to put out these rubber fake weapons and fake tanks.

398
00:33:13,920 --> 00:33:16,620
It's just enough so they can be seen from the air.

399
00:33:18,420 --> 00:33:20,460
Another key element of the operation

400
00:33:20,461 --> 00:33:23,860
was spreading misinformation through German spies in England

401
00:33:23,861 --> 00:33:27,180
who had been exposed and coerced into becoming double agents.

402
00:33:28,540 --> 00:33:29,820
The British said, you guys,

403
00:33:30,400 --> 00:33:32,960
it's either going to be a bullet right into your brain

404
00:33:32,961 --> 00:33:34,380
or you're going to work for us.

405
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:36,880
And these guys said, well, we'll work for you.

406
00:33:38,460 --> 00:33:41,980
The double agents helped convince the Germans of what they already suspected.

407
00:33:42,740 --> 00:33:45,680
The Allies, led by Patton, would attack at Calais.

408
00:33:46,100 --> 00:33:48,880
So how successful was Fortitude? It couldn't have been more successful.

409
00:33:50,080 --> 00:33:51,520
The Allies could not have asked for more.

410
00:33:56,680 --> 00:33:59,760
All the while, the Germans weren't helping their own cause,

411
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:02,800
as they'd begun to believe their own propaganda films

412
00:34:02,801 --> 00:34:05,760
that celebrated the impenetrability of the Atlantic Wall.

413
00:34:05,761 --> 00:34:10,520
Great photographs taken of the huge guns.

414
00:34:10,900 --> 00:34:14,700
Of course, there weren't very many of them, but they photographed the same guns many times.

415
00:34:15,140 --> 00:34:19,660
They made sure the Allies were aware of it and hoped to frighten them, which did not work,

416
00:34:20,100 --> 00:34:27,840
but certainly had great value inside Germany to show how strong the system was to hold off the Americans and the British.

417
00:34:28,760 --> 00:34:33,660
Meanwhile, General Eisenhower and the Allies were making final preparations for D-Day.

418
00:34:33,661 --> 00:34:40,660
Allied troops would land at Normandy's beaches on the morning of June 5, 1944.

419
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:49,660
The British would land at Sword and Gold, the Canadians at Juneau, and the Americans at Omaha and Utah.

420
00:34:49,661 --> 00:34:57,660
Every logistic of the most ambitious invasion plan in human history was falling into place, except for the weather.

421
00:34:57,661 --> 00:35:00,660
Storms were brewing in the English Channel.

422
00:35:00,661 --> 00:35:06,140
Seas were so high that Eisenhower feared the armada would be destroyed before it ever got to France.

423
00:35:09,160 --> 00:35:14,520
And so with everything he heard, he polled his commanders.

424
00:35:15,340 --> 00:35:16,360
Marty always said go.

425
00:35:17,580 --> 00:35:20,520
Navy commanders said no. The Air Force commanders said no.

426
00:35:22,380 --> 00:35:25,240
So he deferred the invasion.

427
00:35:26,660 --> 00:35:29,820
Ships were called back, planes taken off alert.

428
00:35:29,821 --> 00:35:35,160
But if the weather cleared up, Eisenhower would order them back across the channel to France.

429
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:41,940
If not, he would have to postpone the invasion until June 19th to coincide with the next high tide.

430
00:35:45,380 --> 00:35:51,000
On the morning of June 5th, Eisenhower's staff meteorologist promised the weather would improve.

431
00:35:52,080 --> 00:35:54,400
But gray clouds darkened the skies.

432
00:35:54,520 --> 00:35:56,820
He was driving up in a driving rain.

433
00:35:56,821 --> 00:36:03,180
And he said, how in the world am I ever going to order an invasion under these conditions?

434
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:04,740
So he polled.

435
00:36:05,800 --> 00:36:07,420
Once again, Bonnie always said, go.

436
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:10,020
The Navy said, okay.

437
00:36:10,160 --> 00:36:12,080
And the Air Force said, maybe.

438
00:36:12,820 --> 00:36:13,840
So he said, we'll go.

439
00:36:15,980 --> 00:36:18,160
Eisenhower knew there would be no turning back.

440
00:36:18,960 --> 00:36:23,240
More than 150,000 men headed to France to pierce the Atlantic Wall

441
00:36:23,241 --> 00:36:25,380
and push the Germans back to Berlin.

442
00:36:26,820 --> 00:36:33,900
As the sun set on the English Channel, the Germans had no inkling that the largest invasion

443
00:36:33,901 --> 00:36:41,060
force in history was on its way to Normandy.

444
00:36:41,061 --> 00:36:46,060
The term D-Day was first used by the United States Army during World War I to refer to

445
00:36:46,061 --> 00:36:49,220
the first day of an upcoming battle.

446
00:36:49,221 --> 00:36:56,620
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

447
00:36:56,621 --> 00:36:58,300
June 5, 1944.

448
00:36:59,660 --> 00:37:02,380
After another day bolstering the Atlantic Wall,

449
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:05,320
German soldiers retired to their quarters.

450
00:37:07,180 --> 00:37:09,360
As they slept in the wall that night,

451
00:37:09,860 --> 00:37:12,840
they had no idea that the invasion had already begun.

452
00:37:15,540 --> 00:37:18,500
Midnight, June 6, D-Day.

453
00:37:19,380 --> 00:37:21,220
Soldiers began dropping from the skies

454
00:37:21,221 --> 00:37:23,520
behind the coastal fortifications of Normandy.

455
00:37:24,860 --> 00:37:26,440
Among their most critical assignments,

456
00:37:26,620 --> 00:37:29,600
were securing bridges and destroying communication lines.

457
00:37:30,800 --> 00:37:34,740
Paratroopers and glider troops landed well behind the German lines,

458
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:37,020
but the wall had answers for them too.

459
00:37:37,680 --> 00:37:42,280
There were poles set in the fields called Rommel's Asparagus

460
00:37:42,281 --> 00:37:43,780
to smash up the gliders.

461
00:37:46,840 --> 00:37:50,860
Their fields were flooded wherever the Germans could do that

462
00:37:50,861 --> 00:37:53,040
in order to prevent the troops from landing there.

463
00:37:53,041 --> 00:37:57,780
and at great loss they did succeed in landing behind the wall.

464
00:37:59,940 --> 00:38:04,120
At dawn, six hours after the commando raids began,

465
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:08,500
the Germans stationed along the Atlantic Wall found themselves confronted

466
00:38:08,501 --> 00:38:11,200
by the greatest armada ever assembled.

467
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:18,600
Suddenly, Normandy shook as the Allied fleet unleashed the full might of its firepower.

468
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:21,640
Naval gunfire was massive.

469
00:38:21,641 --> 00:38:28,320
The Germans obviously had great difficulties living through that and surviving it psychologically.

470
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:34,160
The gunfire included battleship guns up to 14-inch shells weighing half a ton.

471
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:39,600
There were cruiser shells, there were destroyer gunfire, rocket fire.

472
00:38:40,060 --> 00:38:44,920
The beaches were blanketed, but the deep bunkers were largely safe.

473
00:38:47,160 --> 00:38:49,300
At Soar, where the British would land,

474
00:38:49,301 --> 00:38:51,660
The heavy bombardment was a success.

475
00:38:52,640 --> 00:38:55,100
But on other beaches, especially Omaha,

476
00:38:55,840 --> 00:38:59,440
the defenses the Allied soldiers now approached were still intact.

477
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:04,940
They got just clobbered, just clobbered by German machine guns,

478
00:39:05,060 --> 00:39:09,580
by German mortars, by German 88s that were flying across the beach.

479
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:12,260
And they just took horrendous casualties.

480
00:39:14,480 --> 00:39:17,260
Allied soldiers approaching the shore in Higgins boats

481
00:39:17,261 --> 00:39:19,380
were caught in the hail of German gunfire.

482
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:25,520
A shell hit the front end of the boat that I was on

483
00:39:25,521 --> 00:39:27,900
and knocked the ramp right off.

484
00:39:28,180 --> 00:39:30,400
Lieutenant Dixon was sitting in front of me,

485
00:39:30,840 --> 00:39:33,400
and he went down, and the one in the left compartment

486
00:39:34,020 --> 00:39:35,700
was bleeding around the head,

487
00:39:36,620 --> 00:39:38,840
and he was screaming, get off of the boat.

488
00:39:40,180 --> 00:39:45,380
And Lieutenant Leibowitz, he was the first one I saw got hit.

489
00:39:45,381 --> 00:39:47,960
he was a lieutenant on my boat.

490
00:39:49,060 --> 00:39:50,540
He fell in the water.

491
00:39:50,720 --> 00:39:52,120
I had to see it up out of water

492
00:39:52,121 --> 00:39:54,120
until he died.

493
00:39:55,240 --> 00:39:56,360
Close to shore,

494
00:39:56,980 --> 00:39:59,100
deadly beach obstacles added to the mayhem.

495
00:40:00,280 --> 00:40:01,840
And we got into the area

496
00:40:01,841 --> 00:40:03,520
where there were the telegraph poles

497
00:40:03,521 --> 00:40:06,480
and these had teller mines on them.

498
00:40:07,020 --> 00:40:09,100
We dropped down in that area

499
00:40:09,101 --> 00:40:12,240
because there was machine guns firing in the area

500
00:40:12,241 --> 00:40:13,200
and men were being hit.

501
00:40:13,201 --> 00:40:20,200
The soldiers who survived long enough to hit the shore encountered a barrage of bullets from German machine gun nests.

502
00:40:20,201 --> 00:40:24,200
People followed all around you.

503
00:40:24,201 --> 00:40:28,200
Well, first sergeant, he got hit.

504
00:40:28,201 --> 00:40:31,200
He turned around and went back to the water.

505
00:40:31,201 --> 00:40:34,200
Said, they'll never take me alive.

506
00:40:34,201 --> 00:40:36,200
Which they didn't.

507
00:40:36,201 --> 00:40:40,200
At Omaha, there was no place to retreat.

508
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:43,880
retreat. The Americans had no choice but to move forward.

509
00:40:45,160 --> 00:40:52,326
One of the guys started angling towards us across the beach, and they shot him.

510
00:40:52,327 --> 00:40:53,027
I saw

511
00:40:53,028 --> 00:40:58,580
him hit the dirt, and he was screaming, you know, for a medic, and one of our medics went

512
00:40:58,581 --> 00:41:00,500
over to help him, and they shot the medic.

513
00:41:01,060 --> 00:41:04,741
I didn't take more than a few steps when I was suddenly struck.

514
00:41:04,742 --> 00:41:06,260
Everything in the backpack

515
00:41:06,261 --> 00:41:10,300
was shattered with machine gun bullets.

516
00:41:10,920 --> 00:41:14,620
Wherever soldiers turned, obstacles and booby traps awaited.

517
00:41:15,520 --> 00:41:16,920
He stepped on the wire.

518
00:41:17,720 --> 00:41:20,640
It set it off that bouncing bedding.

519
00:41:21,120 --> 00:41:23,340
It's got everything in the book in it.

520
00:41:23,480 --> 00:41:27,700
It's got razor blades, any kind of metal that they could find to put in there.

521
00:41:28,420 --> 00:41:33,380
I was hit through throat, shoulder, ear, and it was pretty rough.

522
00:41:33,381 --> 00:41:39,120
Amidst all the carnage, the soldiers at Omaha reached the bluffs overlooking the shore.

523
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:48,560
And when I got up to the top, I looked back, and all I could see was 2,000 ships.

524
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:55,020
And all I could think was, well, these damn fool Germans, I wouldn't stay here because we're going to kick their ass.

525
00:41:57,060 --> 00:42:03,240
And then we saw the results of D-Day, and it was appalling.

526
00:42:03,380 --> 00:42:10,380
The bodies were in the surf, rolling in the tide.

527
00:42:10,381 --> 00:42:14,380
And it was a frightful sight.

528
00:42:14,381 --> 00:42:25,380
The assault on the Atlantic wall at Normandy on June 6th resulted in some 4,900 Allied casualties.

529
00:42:25,381 --> 00:42:31,380
But ultimately, the wall had been a failure.

530
00:42:31,381 --> 00:42:35,240
The Allies had breached the mighty barrier in just 14 hours.

531
00:42:36,480 --> 00:42:38,720
By August, they were in Paris.

532
00:42:39,380 --> 00:42:43,140
By spring 1945, they'd crossed the Rhine into Germany.

533
00:42:44,100 --> 00:42:45,860
You want to know why the Atlantic Wall failed?

534
00:42:46,540 --> 00:42:49,340
Because of the people who went against it.

535
00:42:50,360 --> 00:42:51,480
That's why it failed.

536
00:42:54,820 --> 00:42:57,820
During the six decades since the end of World War II,

537
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:00,280
much of the wall was dismantled.

538
00:43:00,281 --> 00:43:04,160
and most of the guns and steel salvaged for scrap metal.

539
00:43:06,260 --> 00:43:08,020
Bunkers and obstacles were removed

540
00:43:08,021 --> 00:43:11,160
by those wanting to erase the scars from this barbaric period.

541
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:15,420
But there are a few places where the twisted visions

542
00:43:15,421 --> 00:43:18,020
of a world gone mad have been preserved.

543
00:43:19,380 --> 00:43:23,540
On the coast of Belgium, a section of the wall is now a museum.

544
00:43:24,640 --> 00:43:26,860
It's a piece of history for us.

545
00:43:26,861 --> 00:43:31,240
even if it's a dark page of history,

546
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,420
because many people suffered during the war.

547
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:38,820
And for that purpose, we want to keep it and preserve it

548
00:43:38,821 --> 00:43:43,380
and show it to everybody, especially to the young people

549
00:43:43,381 --> 00:43:48,020
who do not always realize that liberty has a certain cost.

550
00:43:51,380 --> 00:43:53,800
Nowhere is that precious cost more apparent

551
00:43:53,801 --> 00:43:57,020
than on this hallowed ground above the beaches of Normandy.

552
00:44:01,040 --> 00:44:05,180
As it turned out, the Atlantic Wall marked the end of an era.

553
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:11,780
Modern air power and weaponry soon made this kind of fixed fortification obsolete.

554
00:44:14,120 --> 00:44:18,080
But what remains of the wall serves as a potent reminder

555
00:44:18,081 --> 00:44:20,720
of the tenuous nature of freedom.

